<article class="env-definition" id="07GL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07GL">Definition <span data-tag="07GL">23.2.1</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $I$ be an ideal of $A$. A collection of maps $\gamma _ n : I \to I$, $n > 0$ is called a <em>divided power structure</em> on $I$ if for all $n \geq 0$, $m > 0$, $x, y \in I$, and $a \in A$ we have </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\gamma _1(x) = x$, we also set $\gamma _0(x) = 1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\gamma _ n(x)\gamma _ m(x) = \frac{(n + m)!}{n! m!} \gamma _{n + m}(x)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\gamma _ n(ax) = a^ n \gamma _ n(x)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\gamma _ n(x + y) = \sum _{i = 0, \ldots , n} \gamma _ i(x)\gamma _{n - i}(y)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\gamma _ n(\gamma _ m(x)) = \frac{(nm)!}{n! (m!)^ n} \gamma _{nm}(x)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>